myths.Agent_2:
In the beginning, the Great Weaver gave two siblings, Sol and Luna, a pile of golden seeds. "If you plant these in your own soil," the Weaver said, "they remain but meager seeds. But if you cast them across the divide into the garden of your kin, they shall triple in number, blossoming into a harvest of abundance."

Sol, driven by a cold, hollow fear, hoarded his seeds, clutching them until they withered in his grip. He remained hungry, for his soil was barren. Luna, however, looked across the divide and chose to trust her brother. She cast her entire handful into his plot. Instantly, the earth surged, and the seeds tripled in brilliance. Sol, witnessing this miracle of growth, realized that his sister’s faith was the true catalyst for prosperity. He did not succumb to greed; he gathered the golden harvest and returned a generous portion to Luna, ensuring both were well-fed. They learned that the earth responds only to the open hand. To hold back is to invite scarcity, but to reach across the gap is to invite multiplication. They became the architects of a cycle where giving was the key to receiving, and the garden flourished forevermore.

